     iissccssii..ccoonnff — ISCSI daemon configuration file

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The iscsid(8) daemon implements the Internet Small Computer Systems In‐
     terface as described in RFC 3720.

     The iissccssii..ccoonnff configuration file is divided into the following main sec‐
     tions:

     _M_a_c_r_o_s
           Definitions of variables that can be used later, simplifying the
           configuration file.

     _G_l_o_b_a_l _c_o_n_f_i_g_u_r_a_t_i_o_n
           Global settings for iscsid(8).

     _T_a_r_g_e_t _c_o_n_f_i_g_u_r_a_t_i_o_n
           Target-specific parameters.

     With the exception of macros, the sections should be grouped and appear
     in iissccssii..ccoonnff in the order shown above.

     The current line can be extended over multiple lines using a backslash
     (‘\’).  Comments can be put anywhere in the file using a hash mark (‘#’),
     and extend to the end of the current line.  Care should be taken when
     commenting out multi-line text: the comment is effective until the end of
     the entire block.

     Argument names not beginning with a letter, digit, or underscore must be
     quoted.

     Additional configuration files can be included with the iinncclluuddee keyword,
     for example:

           include "/etc/iscsi-target.conf"

   MMaaccrrooss
     A macro is defined with a command of the form _n_a_m_e=_v_a_l_u_e.  The macro _n_a_m_e
     can contain letters, digits, and underscores and cannot be a reserved
     word (for example, ttaarrggeett or ppoorrtt).  Within unquoted arguments, the
     string $_n_a_m_e is later expanded to _v_a_l_u_e.

     For example:

           target1="1.2.3.4"
           target "disk1" {
                   targetaddr $target1
           }

   GGlloobbaall ccoonnffiigguurraattiioonn
     There are a few settings that affect the operation of the iscsid(8) dae‐
     mon globally.

     iissiidd (oouuii|eenn|rraanndd) _b_a_s_e _q_u_a_l
             The Initiator Session ID iissiidd specifies the initiator part of the
             Session Identifier.  It is set during startup of iscsid(8) and is
             used for session reinstatement.  By default a random iissiidd is gen‐
             erated on startup.  The random rraanndd form has a 24-bit random num‐
             ber as _b_a_s_e and a 16-bit _q_u_a_l qualifier.  The oouuii format uses a
             22-bit _b_a_s_e OUI and a 24-bit _q_u_a_l qualifier.  The IANA enterprise
             number format eenn uses the 24-bit enterprise number in _b_a_s_e and a
             16-bit _q_u_a_l qualifier.

   TTaarrggeett ccoonnffiigguurraattiioonn
     iscsid(8) establishes TCP connections to iSCSI targets.  Each target is
     specified by a _t_a_r_g_e_t section, which allows properties to be set specifi‐
     cally for that target:

           target disk1 {
                   targetaddr 10.0.0.2
                   targetname "iqn.1994-04.org.netbsd.iscsi-target:target:0"
           }

     There are several target properties:

     [ddiissaabblleedd|eennaabblleedd]
             No session will be established to the target if ddiissaabblleedd is set.
             The default value is eennaabblleedd.

     [ddiissccoovveerryy|nnoorrmmaall]
             Define the type of session that will be established.  It is pos‐
             sible to initiate a special ddiissccoovveerryy session to a target to enu‐
             merate the available volumes.

     iinniittiiaattoorraaddddrr _a_d_d_r [iinneett|iinneett66]
             When iscsid(8) initiates the TCP connection to the target system,
             it normally does not bind to a specific IP address and port.  If
             an iinniittiiaattoorraaddddrr is given, it binds to this address first.

     iinniittiiaattoorrnnaammee _s_t_r_i_n_g
             Specify the iinniittiiaattoorrnnaammee used to connect to the remote target.
             The iinniittiiaattoorrnnaammee can be used to restrict access to a target.  If
             not given, iscsid(8) will use _i_q_n_._1_9_9_5_-_1_1_._o_r_g_._o_p_e_n_b_s_d_._i_s_c_s_i_d as
             default.

     ttaarrggeettaaddddrr _a_d_d_r [iinneett|iinneett66] [ppoorrtt _n_u_m]
             Define the target IP address which iscsid(8) should connect to.
             By default port 3260 will be used.

     ttaarrggeettnnaammee _s_t_r_i_n_g
             The ttaarrggeettnnaammee specifies which LUN or disk should be requested by
             iscsid(8).  It is possible to enumerate remote systems with a
             ddiissccoovveerryy session.

